          </p>

          <h3 id="hosted-data">8.4 Code and Data You Send to the Hosted Services</h3>
          <p>
            When you send code to the Legesher Translation API, the code and its translation are processed in memory
            to answer the request. They are <strong>not written to logs and not used to train models</strong>, and
            apart from the names described below they are <strong>not kept after the request ends</strong>.
          </p>
          <p>The Hosted Services do keep some things about your use:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>A usage record for each request.</strong> It says who made the request (your account or key or,
              without an account, a salted hash of your network address), when, with which tool, in which languages,
              how large the request was and whether it succeeded. If you chose to name a repository, it holds a salted
              hash of that name. It holds none of your code.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Service logs and limits.</strong> Like any online service, the Hosted Services log requests, not
              their content, and count requests against the limits in Section 16.1. Without an account, the limit is
              counted against your network address.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Your account.</strong> Your account record, your API keys (stored as hashes, not as the keys
              themselves) and, on a paid plan, the state of your subscription.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Names, for features that use a language model.</strong>
              <span data-review="processor">On plans that include them, features that use a language model send the
              parts of your code that need translating in context (comments, docstrings, text strings and the names
              you chose) to a model provider, which processes them to return the translation. They are not used to
              train models.</span> So that a name is translated the same way from one request to the next, the service
              keeps the names from a repository you identify, together with their translations. They are kept for
              your account only and are not shared with other customers.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            <span data-review="jurisdiction">The same handling applies when a school or another institution directs
            its students to the Hosted Services. The Hosted Services are not directed at children under 13, or under 16
            where the law sets that age.</span>
          </p>
          <p>
